WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.700
So the blue one in the middle of this series
is argon, so again this is a

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.430
very nice glass tube that we’ve had made
with a low pressure of argon

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.169
inside. We’ve put a potential across from
the two ends, here and here,

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:15.469
and this excites all the argon molecules inside
so you can see this

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:19.880
wonderful characteristic eerie blue glow coming
off from the argon.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.990
Argon is one of the noble gases or so-called
rare gases. It’s not really

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:30.250
quite so rare. It consists in really quite
a high percentage, nearly one

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.040
percent of the atmosphere.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.490
If I look at the one in the middle I can see
lots and lots of lines in the

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.500
blue and the green region and these are all
specific to the individual

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.840
atoms that are in the tubes themselves.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.340
But it’s very un-reactive so early chemists
found it quite difficult to

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:53.570
identify, in fact it was only Sir William
Ramsey that identified the argon in

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.809
1894 and he was in fact the first person in
Britain to win a Nobel Prize for

00:00:58.809 --> 00:00:59.890
chemistry.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:04.969
So if we turn off the power to this, we turn
off the potential and then we

00:01:04.969 --> 00:01:08.329
don’t excite the molecules so what we’d
see is just a clear glass tube

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:12.090
with no visible sign of anything inside. And
only when we turn on the

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.289
potential that we excite the molecules, so
they emit and give us these

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:16.530
really quite wonderful colours.

